  {
    "id": "cvpr26-96ac66b9a82d",
    "extraction": {
      "id": "cvpr26-96ac66b9a82d",
      "figure": "Figure 2",
      "caption": "Figure 2. Overview of SelecTKD, a “propose-and-verify” framework for selective token-level knowledge distillation. Left: Dataset Gen- eration. Both teacher p(x, y) and student qθ(x, y) can produce responses, enabling on- and off-policy data. Middle: Model Prediction and Token Sampling. Two variants are supported: (a) Greedy Top-k: the student proposes a greedy token ˆyt (argmax) and the teacher returns its Top-k set; (b) Non-greedy Spec-k: the student samples k candidate tokens and the teacher is queried only on these candidates to compute acceptance indices following speculative sampling [6, 31]. Right: Verification and Loss Computation. A token is accepted if it is in the teacher’s Top-k (greedy) or passes the speculative acceptance test (non-greedy). Accepted tokens receive full loss; rejected tokens are masked or down-weighted by β. The loss is objective-agnostic and works with KL, RKL, SKL, and SRKL. This design focuses learning on reliable, teacher-aligned tokens and yields a stable, curriculum-like training dynamic.",
