  {
    "id": "cvpr26-fa813ce84572",
    "extraction": {
      "figure": "Figure 2",
      "caption": "Figure 2 : Art3D Framework. Our pipeline takes a 2D Left View as input. 1. Geometric Feature Extraction (Frozen): ‘DepthNet’ extracts geometric features and the inverse depth map, ‘geometric canvas ( i ​ z iz )’. 2. Artistic Blueprint & Masks (Frozen, Data Construction Stage): During the data construction stage (no training or inference required), ‘StereoNet’ estimates the target artistic blueprint ( d L d^{L} ) and provides a valid pixel mask ( M v ​ a ​ l ​ i ​ d M_{valid} ) via its left-right consistency check. Simultaneously, ‘Lang-SAM’ analyzes the Left View to generate the local effects mask ( M l ​ o ​ c ​ a ​ l M_{local} ). The final global style mask ( M g ​ l ​ o ​ b ​ a ​ l M_{global} ) is then derived from valid regions that are not part of the local effects (i.e., M v ​ a ​ l ​ i ​ d ⋅ ( 1 − M l ​ o ​ c ​ a ​ l ) M_{valid}\\cdot(1-M_{local}) ). 3. CameraNet (Trainable): This network takes features and i ​ z iz to synthesize the virtual camera parameters ( v ​ s , v ​ t vs,vt ), which are pixel-level tensors , and a preliminary virtual right disparity map ( d ^ R \\hat{d}^{R} ). The virtual left disparity map ( d ^ L \\hat{d}^{L} ) is then constructed as i ​ z × v ​ s + v ​ t iz\\times vs+vt . 4. Dual-Path Supervision: Our core ‘ ℒ A ​ r ​ t \\mathcal{L}_{Art} ’ loss guides the learning of v ​ s , v ​ t vs,vt by comparing d ^ L \\hat{d}^{L} with d L d^{L} , weighted by M g ​ l ​ o ​ b ​ a ​ l M_{global} (for global style) and M l ​ o ​ c ​ a ​ l M_{local} (for local effects). An additional left-right consistency check loss further refines d ^ R \\hat{d}^{R} . 5. Virtual Right View Synthesis: The final d ^ R \\hat{d}^{R} , combined with the original Left View , can be used to generate the Virtual Right View via standard warping techniques.\nFor more details on the loss functions, please refer to Sec. 3 .",
      "image_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2603.05906v1/framework1_art3d.jpg",
