    "extraction": {
      "id": "cvpr26-c6cfe97a1e7e",
      "figure": "Figure 3",
      "caption": "Figure 3. Overview of the proposed Causal-OT framework. The model takes source (Xs, Ys) and target (Xt) time-series as input. (i) Causal Graph Construction: Granger causality graphs (Gs, Gt) are extracted from the raw time-series (In figure black arrow indicates inter-variables causal relation and red arrow indicates non-causal relation). (ii) Feature Extraction: A shared feature extractor fθ maps data into latent features (Zs, Zt). (iii) Causal Alignment: An Optimal Transport (OT) loss, LOT , aligns the domains. The transport cost matrix C incorporates both feature distance and causal graph distance DG. (iv) Classification & Pseudo-Labeling: A classifier hϕ is trained on source data (Lsrc) and generates pseudo-labels for confident target samples (LP L). (v) Optimization: The total loss is a weighted sum of the three components, used to jointly update the model parameters θ and ϕ.",
